data.content:
Yes — MSTest v3.8+ supports **typed `DynamicData`** using tuples, eliminating the `object[]` boxing:

```csharp
[TestMethod]
[DynamicData(nameof(TestData))]
public void Validate_ReturnsExpected(string input, bool expected)
{
    var result = Validator.IsValid(input);
    Assert.AreEqual(expected, result);
}

public static IEnumerable<(string Input, bool Expected)> TestData =>
[
    ("valid@email.com", true),
    ("invalid", false),
    ("", false),
];
```

**What changed:**
- `IEnumerable<object[]>` → `IEnumerable<(string, bool)>` — gives compile-time type checking
- Array literals `["valid@email.com", true]` → tuple literals `("valid@email.com", true)`

If you have a single parameter, you can also use `IEnumerable<string>` directly (no tuple needed). This feature requires **MSTest 3.8+** (`Microsoft.Testing.Framework`).
